\section{Motivation}
Over the last 10.000 years, humans have reshaped large portions of the earths surface by cutting down trees and replaced them with crops, substituting forests with farmlands. In 2013 a database of forest coverage and deforestation for entire earth during during the period 2000-2013 was published (ref), and it has been updated in 2023. The data points in this database represent very small rectangles, approximately $30\times30 \textrm{m}^2$. If you want to explain why deforestation takes place, or the social consequences of deforestation, you need to put the deforestation data in context, and for this purpose you need data aggregated to some socially relevant area unit. A natural choice for such areas are the administrative areas defined by each country. Aggregating time series data for 152 billion tiny rectangles to time series data for 18956 administrative areas is an essential step towards analyses of causes and consequences of deforestation, and this document both serves as the canonical source for how it was done, and as a manual for how to use the aggregated database.

\section{Aim}
The aim of this article is to document a dataproduct so that it can be used correctly. The data product contains aggregated time-series data on forest coverage and deforestation in between year 2000 and 2023 in 18956 administrative areas in Latin America, Africa and South Asia. The database can be also reconstructed from scratch by following the instructions laid out in the source code of this document.



In this database, and in this document at large, \emph{forest coverage} has a precise definition: the proportion of the area that is covered by organic structures (trees) that reflects sunlight at least 5 meters above the ground. Deforestation is defined as the decrease in forestation. If an 70 per cent of an area was covered forest at time t0, and at time t1 65 per cent of the area is covered by forest, the deforestation of that area between time t0 and t1 was 5. The recorded deforestation statistic is this the difference in forestation. If you want to analyse the \emph{relative} deforestation, e.g. by calculating how large percentage of the existing forest at time t0 was lost at time t1, you have to first derive the forestation state at t1 and then take the ratio between forestation at t0 and t1.

\section{Requirements}
The program downloads almost all of the necessary data from the internet, but data from the World Bank is currently read from local files, so the path to these needs to be set in the variable \texttt{country.data.path} which should be a path to the \emph{directory} where the following files reside:

\begin{itemize}
\item "API\_NY.GDP.PCAP.CD\_DS2\_en\_excel\_v2\_5795829.xlsx"
\item "API\_NE.EXP.GNFS.ZS\_DS2\_en\_excel\_v2\_5728793.xlsx"
\item "API\_NV.AGR.TOTL.ZS\_DS2\_en\_excel\_v2\_5728821.xlsx"
\item "API\_SP.POP.1564.TO\_DS2\_en\_excel\_v2\_5731200.xlsx"
\end{itemize}

